WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.305
Hello and welcome. I'm James Murphy.

00:00:02.305 --> 00:00:07.318
In this video,&nbsp;we're talking about which dataclass solution is best for your situation.

00:00:07.318 --> 00:00:10.458
I already have a&nbsp;whole video explaining what dataclasses are.

00:00:10.458 --> 00:00:14.209
But for those of you that haven't seen that video&nbsp;yet, here's the gist.

00:00:14.209 --> 00:00:16.244
Throughout the process of writing code,

00:00:16.244 --> 00:00:21.263
you often create classes that are&nbsp;basically just containers for pieces of data that you've collected.

00:00:21.263 --> 00:00:22.969
It's a good thing to make&nbsp;these containers

00:00:22.969 --> 00:00:26.473
because they can really help people understand the meaning of your code.

00:00:26.473 --> 00:00:28.408
Take a&nbsp;look at this simple init function.

00:00:28.408 --> 00:00:30.966
All I want to do is store an int of float in a string.

00:00:30.966 --> 00:00:35.868
But here, you&nbsp;can see that n, f and s are repeated three times for each of them.

00:00:35.868 --> 00:00:37.843
This is already more repetition&nbsp;than I'd like.

00:00:37.843 --> 00:00:39.819
And it's only going to get worse if I want to add things

00:00:39.819 --> 00:00:43.443
like a hash function or a&nbsp;compare function to this class.

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:46.631
Dataclasses help me get rid of all that boilerplate.

00:00:46.631 --> 00:00:48.389
Here's the&nbsp;same class.

00:00:48.389 --> 00:00:51.327
Notice that there's nothing repeated in the definition of the class.

00:00:51.327 --> 00:00:54.708
I only have n, f,&nbsp;s and their type hints once.

00:00:54.708 --> 00:00:59.004
But this built-in dataclass decorator isn't the only solution that&nbsp;offers similar functionality.

00:00:59.004 --> 00:01:01.638
We're going to look at the different features that each solution has.

00:01:01.638 --> 00:01:05.162
And then also compare how fast they are and how much memory they use.

00:01:05.162 --> 00:01:10.311
If you're looking for the&nbsp;classic no frills dependency free solution for most cases,

00:01:10.311 --> 00:01:12.822
then just stick with the built-in dataclass.

00:01:12.822 --> 00:01:18.834
In my last video, the only reservation that I had about using the built-in dataclass decorator&nbsp;was that it didn't support slots.

00:01:18.834 --> 00:01:20.773
Well, I'm using Python 3.10.

00:01:20.773 --> 00:01:25.727
So, now it's just this easy in order&nbsp;to use slots instead of an instance dictionary.

00:01:25.727 --> 00:01:29.390
The first really good alternative to dataclass&nbsp;is attrs.

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:32.151
Sometimes attrs is a little bit more verbose than dataclasses.

00:01:32.151 --> 00:01:34.948
But it also comes with&nbsp;a little bit more functionality.

00:01:34.948 --> 00:01:38.724
In addition to supporting everything that dataclasses support&nbsp;including slots,

00:01:38.724 --> 00:01:42.525
attrs also supports variable conversions, data validation

00:01:42.525 --> 00:01:46.403
and default factories&nbsp;specifically for mutable arguments.

00:01:46.403 --> 00:01:48.618
Here I specify a converter which is just int.

00:01:48.618 --> 00:01:52.347
So, anything you&nbsp;pass in, it'll pass into int before storing it.

00:01:52.347 --> 00:01:57.422
There are a bunch of built-in validators like&nbsp;checking if something is an instance of the right type at runtime.

00:01:57.422 --> 00:02:02.240
And you can specify default&nbsp;arguments and default factories for mutable things.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.162
Even though you see me using dataclasses in most&nbsp;of my videos,

00:02:05.162 --> 00:02:08.298
I actually use attrs more in real life.

00:02:08.298 --> 00:02:13.448
attrs just has that little bit of extra&nbsp;flexibility that can make a big difference in a real project.

00:02:13.448 --> 00:02:16.765
The next three alternatives are&nbsp;just based off of tuples.

00:02:16.765 --> 00:02:19.701
Whenever I talk about dataclasses, I always get people asking:

00:02:19.701 --> 00:02:22.221
Why&nbsp;can't I just store this information in a tuple?

00:02:22.221 --> 00:02:26.650
Well, of course you can, if you want your object to&nbsp;be treated like a tuple all the time.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:28.992
Tuples are printed in a sensible way by default.

00:02:28.992 --> 00:02:32.080
And they're&nbsp;really fast and they're really memory efficient.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.772
If you just want to store three pieces of data,&nbsp;then a tuple is a good choice.

00:02:35.772 --> 00:02:37.859
If you want to make your code just a little bit more readable

00:02:37.859 --> 00:02:40.436
so you don't have to access everything by index,

00:02:40.436 --> 00:02:42.490
you can use a namedtuple.

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:46.212
A namedtuple from the&nbsp;collections library is a tuple.

00:02:46.212 --> 00:02:48.930
It's a tuple that gives names to each of its elements.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:53.803
And that means&nbsp;that in addition to accessing things by index, you can also access them by name.

00:02:53.803 --> 00:02:56.129
This is a huge win&nbsp;for readability.

00:02:56.129 --> 00:03:00.339
But this syntax for creating the class is a little bit cumbersome not easy to&nbsp;read.

00:03:00.339 --> 00:03:02.880
And there's also no types mentioned here.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.067
If you want the exact same thing as a namedtuple

00:03:05.067 --> 00:03:07.438
but also with static type information available,

00:03:07.438 --> 00:03:10.522
then you should use NamedTuple from the typing&nbsp;library.

00:03:10.522 --> 00:03:13.402
This NamedTuple is a thin wrapper around the other one.

00:03:13.402 --> 00:03:17.764
It's still a tuple and it still&nbsp;behaves exactly the same as the other one.

00:03:17.764 --> 00:03:20.260
The benefit though is that this way of defining the&nbsp;class

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:23.744
is much easier to read and it also leaves a space for the type hints.

00:03:23.744 --> 00:03:28.663
If you care about static&nbsp;typing at all, then you should always prefer this NamedTuple over the other one.

00:03:28.663 --> 00:03:32.315
The main drawbacks&nbsp;to using any of these dataclasses that are based off of tuples

00:03:32.315 --> 00:03:35.061
is that they are based off of tuples.

00:03:35.061 --> 00:03:38.937
Tuples are immutable. So, you can't change any of the elements after a tuple is created.

00:03:38.937 --> 00:03:42.499
You'd have&nbsp;to make a copy of the tuple with a different value in one of the slots.

00:03:42.499 --> 00:03:47.492
That means, you basically&nbsp;have to pay the cost of an instance creation every time you want to change something.

00:03:47.492 --> 00:03:52.048
The other&nbsp;main reservation you should have about using these tuples is type safety.

00:03:52.048 --> 00:03:56.435
You're not going to get any&nbsp;kind of error if you try to mix this kind of tuple and a raw untyped tuple

00:03:56.435 --> 00:03:59.661
even if you do operations&nbsp;that semantically might not make sense

00:03:59.661 --> 00:04:02.139
like using plus or less than.

00:04:02.139 --> 00:04:05.133
This is somewhat mitigated if&nbsp;you're actually doing static type checking.

00:04:05.133 --> 00:04:06.951
But you should still be aware.

00:04:06.951 --> 00:04:10.749
I might as well include dict&nbsp;in here because this is what a lot of beginners do.

00:04:10.749 --> 00:04:13.999
There's no type safety or even type information&nbsp;at all.

00:04:13.999 --> 00:04:17.862
And accessing things by string is really really error prone.

00:04:17.862 --> 00:04:20.200
I know it's really convenient&nbsp;to write something like this

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:24.041
especially for testing or the first time that you're looking at&nbsp;a problem.

00:04:24.041 --> 00:04:29.420
But in my experience, using a dictionary this way is too error prone for my level&nbsp;of comfort.

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:34.948
If this structure of having an n, f and s is something that's going to be used&nbsp;many times throughout your application

00:04:34.948 --> 00:04:39.478
and not just confined to a single function, then you&nbsp;should really be using a class.

00:04:39.478 --> 00:04:44.369
A very slight upgrade in readability from a dictionary is&nbsp;to use a SimpleNamespace.

00:04:44.369 --> 00:04:46.434
SimpleNamespace is just like object

00:04:46.434 --> 00:04:50.880
except it allows you to set&nbsp;attributes on it at runtime, whereas object doesn't

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:53.328
Its use is primarily to avoid this anti-pattern

00:04:53.328 --> 00:04:58.686
where you just create a class for the sake of creating an empty class that you can put&nbsp;attributes on.

00:04:58.686 --> 00:05:03.423
And by popular demand, the last choice that we'll be looking at is pydantic.

00:05:03.423 --> 00:05:05.729
Now originally, I wasn't even going to talk about this.

00:05:05.729 --> 00:05:09.680
But in my dataclasses video,&nbsp;I got a ton of comments about pydantic.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:15.255
pydantic is a very opinionated library built for&nbsp;doing a specific task:

00:05:15.255 --> 00:05:16.577
Parsing

00:05:16.577 --> 00:05:20.455
By default, pydantic will try to convert things into these types

00:05:20.455 --> 00:05:23.066
when&nbsp;you construct this object or set an attribute.

00:05:23.066 --> 00:05:26.002
And it will check those types at runtime.

00:05:26.002 --> 00:05:28.758
One of the&nbsp;common problems that you get when you're parsing data

00:05:28.758 --> 00:05:31.169
is that the data you're reading might not&nbsp;be valid

00:05:31.169 --> 00:05:33.157
or it might not be in the right format.

00:05:33.157 --> 00:05:34.959
So, converting things at runtime

00:05:34.959 --> 00:05:37.914
and checking&nbsp;types and checking conversions at runtime

00:05:37.914 --> 00:05:41.812
makes a lot of sense for the specific task of parsing.

00:05:41.812 --> 00:05:47.760
However, doing all of this at runtime is a huge waste of resources, both time and memory,

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.242
if parsing is&nbsp;not your goal.

00:05:50.242 --> 00:05:55.593
If this data structure of an int, float and a string is something that's internal&nbsp;to my application

00:05:55.593 --> 00:06:01.047
and my application is primarily responsible for creating these data structures

00:06:01.047 --> 00:06:03.840
and modifying them and doing whatever with them,

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.766
then it would be a huge waste of resources to do&nbsp;runtime checking.

00:06:08.766 --> 00:06:12.679
It would be better and probably even easier to do static type checking

00:06:12.679 --> 00:06:16.240
and to&nbsp;catch any type errors before the program even runs.

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:21.870
So, in my mind, I don't really see pydantic models&nbsp;as an alternative to dataclasses.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:24.728
They serve a very different and specific purpose.

00:06:24.728 --> 00:06:29.236
If you are&nbsp;doing parsing, like potentially parsing json from a web api,

00:06:29.236 --> 00:06:32.781
then pydantic is a great choice.&nbsp;It's very good at what it does.

00:06:32.781 --> 00:06:35.277
It's just not a general purpose thing.

00:06:35.277 --> 00:06:38.640
And we're going to see that&nbsp;performance hit in the graphs that are coming up.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:42.040
Okay, time to take a step back and look at the&nbsp;overall picture.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:43.120
First up, speed.

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:45.457
How long does it take to create an instance,

00:06:45.457 --> 00:06:48.066
how long does it&nbsp;take to access an attribute on the instance

00:06:48.066 --> 00:06:50.383
and how long does it take to set an attribute?

00:06:50.383 --> 00:06:54.477
These&nbsp;are the creation times for each of the different classes in nanoseconds.

00:06:54.477 --> 00:06:56.825
So, these are all very small.

00:06:56.825 --> 00:07:02.505
However, you can see pydantic is losing by a lot because it's doing this runtime checking.

00:07:02.505 --> 00:07:03.813
That's a&nbsp;huge price to pay.

00:07:03.813 --> 00:07:07.511
So, let's go ahead and zoom in on all the rest of the things.

00:07:07.511 --> 00:07:11.230
There's a little bit of&nbsp;variability. But there's two pretty clear winners.

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:16.450
Obviously, the built-in tuple and built-in&nbsp;dictionary get preferential treatment in the language.

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:19.189
Tuple and dict are written primarily&nbsp;in C.

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:21.634
Everything else has to go through Python.

00:07:21.634 --> 00:07:24.525
That means tuple gets to sit nicely around&nbsp;30 nanoseconds.

00:07:24.525 --> 00:07:27.304
And everything else is taking about 300.

00:07:27.304 --> 00:07:29.307
If you care about this difference in&nbsp;performance,

00:07:29.307 --> 00:07:33.829
then you might need to be writing a C extension or just using C directly.

00:07:33.829 --> 00:07:35.501
So, if you're&nbsp;programming in Python,

00:07:35.501 --> 00:07:37.852
I'd say that all of these should be good choices

00:07:37.852 --> 00:07:41.013
as long as each one&nbsp;has the features that you're looking for.

00:07:41.013 --> 00:07:42.779
Alright, let's look at getattr and setattr.

00:07:42.779 --> 00:07:44.138
The first thing&nbsp;you should notice is that

00:07:44.138 --> 00:07:47.863
the getattr and setattr times are much less than the instance creation times.

00:07:47.863 --> 00:07:50.297
But that doesn't mean that you shouldn't worry about them.

00:07:50.297 --> 00:07:54.145
We often get and set attributes&nbsp;of a single object many many times.

00:07:54.145 --> 00:07:56.774
So, these little times can add up.

00:07:56.774 --> 00:07:58.457
Here's the picture for&nbsp;getting attributes.

00:07:58.457 --> 00:08:02.867
Basically, everything takes the same amount of time except SimpleNameSpace and pydantic.

00:08:02.867 --> 00:08:04.788
It's a similar story for setting attributes.

00:08:04.788 --> 00:08:08.971
Let's go ahead and zoom in&nbsp;so that pydantic isn't dominating everything.

00:08:08.971 --> 00:08:13.133
Here, everything that's mutable, of course,&nbsp;is taking about the same amount of time.

00:08:13.133 --> 00:08:17.462
You can still choose to use an immutable thing&nbsp;even if you want to make changes to the objects.

00:08:17.462 --> 00:08:21.765
Just remember that if you do that, then&nbsp;whenever you want to make a change,

00:08:21.765 --> 00:08:26.259
then you actually pay the creation cost not&nbsp;the setattr cost.

00:08:26.259 --> 00:08:30.370
That's because you basically have to create a new object that has to change data&nbsp;in it.

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:33.876
So, unless the changes that you're making are extremely infrequent

00:08:33.876 --> 00:08:38.260
or if you're using a&nbsp;raw tuple whose creation time is just that low,

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:42.059
then you should probably just prefer to use&nbsp;something that's mutable in the first place.

00:08:42.059 --> 00:08:46.379
On to memory usage. Everything is in bytes and&nbsp;lower is better.

00:08:46.379 --> 00:08:51.949
Everything that used less than 200 bytes is something that is tuple or slots based.

00:08:51.949 --> 00:08:56.353
Everything that took more than 200 bytes used an instance dictionary.

00:08:56.353 --> 00:09:00.358
If you're programming in&nbsp;Python, you're probably not too memory constrained.

00:09:00.358 --> 00:09:02.315
But if you're really creating a lot of objects,

00:09:02.315 --> 00:09:05.261
sometimes it can be useful to save a factor of two.

00:09:05.261 --> 00:09:08.903
This is a big place where attrs and data classes&nbsp;shine.

00:09:08.903 --> 00:09:12.353
You can choose to use slots if you want to conserve that extra memory

00:09:12.353 --> 00:09:16.560
or you can choose to&nbsp;have the convenience of an instance dictionary.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:21.040
Finally, here's the feature matrix to help&nbsp;you decide which one is right for you.

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:25.483
In my opinion, the best overall choice is&nbsp;attrs.

00:09:25.483 --> 00:09:28.640
The most convenient choice is dataclass.

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:33.892
And the best choice for record like data is&nbsp;the capital NamedTuple.

00:09:33.892 --> 00:09:38.905
Of course, if your task is specifically parsing, then use pydantic.

00:09:38.905 --> 00:09:47.600
That's all I've got. Thank you for watching.

